</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/coa/coa/en/item/19487/index.do?q=Crown+Liability+and+Proceedings+Act+section+31+transition">Francis v. Ontario</a></span>
                        - <span class="citation">2021 ONCA 197</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2021-03-31</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Decisions of the Court of Appeal</span>
                    - <span>Civil</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    Further, s. <b>31</b>(4) provides that “[s]ection 11 and the extinguishment of causes of action and dismissal of <b>proceedings</b> under that <b>section</b> apply with respect to <b>proceedings</b> commenced against the <b>Crown</b> or an officer, employee or agent of the <b>Crown</b> before the day this <b>section</b> came into force”. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;(viii) The <b>Proceedings</b> Against the <b>Crown</b> <b>Act</b> [142] One last technical point needs to be addressed with respect to this issue. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Section</b> 5(2) of the PACA simply says that no <b>proceeding</b> can be brought against the <b>Crown</b> “unless a <b>proceeding</b> in tort in respect of such <b>act</b> or omission may be brought against that servant or agent” (emphasis added). 
                </p>
                <div class="spacer">
